# Spelling keys in the body. What is announced is checked in the screen reader runs; here the
# keys must do their work and leave the keyboard in the body.

== Alt+1 replaces the misspelled word the caret moved into
text: I recieve mail
caret: start
keys: ^{RIGHT}
wait: 1.5
keys: %1
wait: 0.8
text-is: I receive mail
focus-is: Message body
typing-works

== Ctrl+F7 selects the next misspelling
text: good words then teh end
caret: start
keys: ^{F7}
wait: 1
selection-is: "teh"
focus-is: Message body
typing-works

== Ctrl+Shift+F7 selects the previous misspelling
text: good teh words then end
caret: end
keys: ^+{F7}
wait: 1
selection-is: "teh"
typing-works

== Ctrl+F7 with nothing misspelled leaves the caret where it was
text: good words here
caret: after "good"
keys: ^{F7}
wait: 1
caret-is: after "good"
typing-works

== F7 opens Check Spelling and Escape returns the keyboard to the body
text: I recieve mail
caret: start
keys: {F7}
wait: 2
front-is: Spelling
keys: {ESC}
wait: 1.2
front-is: New Message
focus-is: Message body
typing-works

== The context menu on a misspelled word offers a suggestion first
text: I recieve mail
caret: after "rec"
keys: +{F10}
wait: 1.2
focus-is: receive
keys: {ENTER}
wait: 1
text-is: I receive mail
typing-works
